---
title: "Shape"
description: "The one-way dependency arrow, the file map, and the size ceiling that keeps the design honest."
---

The codebase has one organising idea, and almost everything else follows from it.

## Dependencies point one way

```{mermaid}
%%| label: fig-arrow
%%| fig-cap: "Every arrow points right. Nothing to the right of a layer is imported by it."
flowchart LR
  C["config.ts<br/><i>the only knobs</i>"] --> D["domain/<br/><i>pure decisions</i>"]
  D --> DB["db/<br/><i>all the SQL</i>"]
  DB --> H["bot/ handlers<br/><i>grammY</i>"]
  H --> R["bot/render.ts<br/><i>pure formatting</i>"]
```

The property this buys is stated once and worth restating: **the scoring model is
never tested through Telegram.** `src/domain/` imports nothing but `config.ts`, so
the streak rule, the ranking rule, the Monday-post decision and the reminder
state machine are all ordinary unit tests with no network, no clock and no
database. `src/db/` needs a Postgres schema but no bot token. Only the handler
files need Telegram at all, and they are deliberately thin.

That thinness is a design constraint, not an accident. When a handler grows a
decision worth testing, the decision moves into `src/domain/` and the handler
calls it. Every file in `src/domain/` got there that way, and each one's header
comment records why.

## The tree

```text
sports-competition/
├── src/                       25 files, 1,958 effective lines
