Two absences worth knowing about, because other documents refer to both as though
they were there.

::: {.callout-warning}
## `prototype/bot-flows.html` has been deleted

It was the clickable screen-by-screen mockup, and it is still cited by `README.md`,
`CLAUDE.md`, `SPEC.md` §7 and §9, and three source comments
(`config.ts:51` on keyboard order, `domain/scoring.ts:13` on the ten-slot bar,
`render.ts:50` on the 45-minute nudge threshold). Every one of those links is now
dead.

It was removed in commit `77507ee`, so it is recoverable:

```sh
git show 77507ee^:prototype/bot-flows.html > /tmp/bot-flows.html
```

The three source comments cite it for **rendering details that are already
implemented**, so nothing is blocked by its absence. But if you are changing the
keyboard order, the progress bar, or the copy, recover it first rather than
guessing at what it specified.
:::

`.superpowers/` also exists at the repository root on the machine this was
developed on, holding the per-phase build ledgers (`progress.md`, `HANDOVER.md`).
Its `.gitignore` contains `*`, so **it is not in the repository and a fresh clone
will not have it.** `CLAUDE.md` describes it because it is the most useful context
on the machine that has it, not because you can expect to find it.

## The file map

::: {.callout-note collapse="true"}
## Every file in `src/`, with its job

**Configuration**

| File | Job |
|---|---|
| `config.ts` | Guilds, member counts, tier minutes, the weekly target, competition dates, the timezone, reminder constants. The only place any of these exist |
| `strings.ts` | Every user-facing string in the product. English, inline, no i18n framework (FR-27) |
| `html.ts` | `escapeHtml`, for interpolating untrusted values into Telegram's HTML parse mode |
| `main.ts` | Startup: connect, migrate, sync guilds, build the bot, start the ticker, install signal handlers |

**`domain/`, pure**

| File | Job |
|---|---|
| `scoring.ts` | Tier minutes, the progress bar, window membership, competition ranking, the weekly streak |
| `scheduling.ts` | The Monday-post decision (FR-20) and the two competition-boundary rules around it |
| `reminders.ts` | The FR-21/FR-22 state machine: what a due user is sent, and what their ignore count becomes |

**`db/`, all the SQL**

| File | Job |
|---|---|
| `client.ts` | The postgres.js connection and `waitForDatabase`'s retry loop |
| `migrate.ts` | Numbered `.sql` files, each applied once inside its own transaction |
| `calendar.ts` | Today, yesterday, the current week's Monday, and the local hour. Every date bucket in the system starts here |
| `tiers.ts` | The config-backed tier-minutes CTE injected into every query that converts tiers to minutes |
| `users.ts` | Registration, guild moves, the reminder-hour write |
| `days.ts` | The log upsert, the guarded undo, and the weekly minutes sum |
| `standings.ts` | The four read queries: guild standings, neighbours, weekly totals, participation |
| `chats.ts` | The guild-to-chat binding and the pin / Monday-post ledger |
| `reminders.ts` | Who is due a reminder, and the writes that record a send, a resume, or a block |

**`bot/`, Telegram**

| File | Job |
|---|---|
| `index.ts` | Builds the bot and installs handlers **in a specific order** |
| `callbacks.ts` | The callback-data codec: one encoder, one decoder, one re-encode assertion |
| `render.ts` | Pure formatting. Every message body is built here |
| `registration.ts` | `/start`, the guild picker, the move-or-stay flow, the reminder question |
| `checkin.ts` | `/log`, the tier buttons, the backdate button, the confirmation and the undo |
| `reports.ts` | `/me` and `/standings` |
| `group.ts` | Binding a group chat to a guild (FR-18) |
| `reminders.ts` | `/remind`, its callbacks, and the batch send called once per tick |
| `ticker.ts` | The 60-second loop: pin refresh, Monday post, reminder pass |
:::

## How the modules actually talk

The layer arrow above is the idea. This is the real thing: every internal import
in `src/`, extracted from the source rather than drawn from memory.

**An arrow means "imports".** `config.ts`, `strings.ts` and `html.ts` are left out
of the graph because nearly everything imports them, and drawing twenty edges into
three boxes would hide the structure that matters. Their relationships are in the
table underneath.

Nodes are shaped by layer: **`bot/`** rectangular, **`db/`** rounded, **`domain/`**
stadium-shaped. The diagram is larger than the page: it scrolls sideways, and
clicking it opens it full size.

```{mermaid}
%%| label: fig-modules
%%| fig-cap: "Every internal import in src/, minus the three universally-imported leaves. An arrow means the source file imports the target. Rectangles are bot/, rounded boxes db/, stadiums domain/."
flowchart LR
  main["main.ts"]

  bindex["bot/index"]
  ticker["bot/ticker"]
  reg["bot/registration"]
  checkin["bot/checkin"]
  reports["bot/reports"]
  group["bot/group"]
  brem["bot/reminders"]
  render["bot/render"]
  cb["bot/callbacks"]

  client("db/client")
  migrate("db/migrate")
  cal("db/calendar")
  tiers("db/tiers")
  users("db/users")
  days("db/days")
  stand("db/standings")
  chats("db/chats")
  drem("db/reminders")

  scoring(["domain/scoring"])
  sched(["domain/scheduling"])
  dremd(["domain/reminders"])

  main --> bindex & ticker & client & migrate & users

  bindex --> reg & checkin & reports & group & brem
  bindex --> drem

  ticker --> cal & chats & stand
  ticker --> sched & scoring
  ticker --> brem & render

  reg --> cb & checkin & users
  checkin --> cb & render & cal & days & users & scoring
  reports --> cb & render & cal & days & stand & users & scoring
  group --> cb & cal & chats
  brem --> cb & checkin & users & drem & dremd

  render --> stand & scoring
  cb --> scoring

  days --> tiers
  stand --> tiers & scoring
  sched --> scoring
```

Read three things off it.

**`domain/` is a sink.** Nothing in it points back out. `scoring.ts` imports only
`config.ts`, which is why the whole scoring model is testable with no database and
no Telegram.

**`db/` never imports `bot/`.** The only db-to-domain edges are `standings.ts`
importing a `WeekTotal` type and `days.ts`/`standings.ts` sharing the tier CTE.
The layering holds in the direction that matters: SQL knows nothing about
Telegram.

**`checkin.ts` is imported by three siblings** (`index.ts`, `registration.ts`,
`bot/reminders.ts`), which is unusual for a handler and deliberate.
`checkInMessage()` lives there and FR-21 requires the daily reminder to send *the*
check-in message, not one that resembles it. The reminder pass has `bot.api` and a
chat id but no `ctx`, so without that shared export there would be two
constructions that agree today and drift later.

### The three omitted leaves

| Module | Imported by | Why it is everywhere |
|---|---|---|
| `config.ts` | 17 of the 25 files | The only source of guilds, tiers, dates and the timezone (FR-25) |
| `strings.ts` | 7 files: `bot/index`, `registration`, `checkin`, `reports`, `group`, `reminders`, `render` | Every user-facing string in one file (FR-27). It imports `config.ts` and `html.ts` itself. Note `ticker.ts` is **not** among them: every string it sends comes through `render.ts` |
| `html.ts` | 2 files: `strings.ts` and `bot/render.ts` | `escapeHtml`, applied to interpolated values only |

## What has no automated tests, and why

`registration.ts`, `checkin.ts`, `reports.ts`, `group.ts`, `reminders.ts` and the
Telegram calls inside `ticker.ts` are **untested by design**. Their acceptance
basis is `docs/SMOKE.md`, run by hand.

This is a real decision rather than a gap someone never got round to. Testing
them means mocking the Bot API, and a mock of Telegram's behaviour is a
restatement of one's assumptions about Telegram, which is exactly the thing most
likely to be wrong. The response was to keep those files thin enough that reading
them is a sufficient check, and to push everything with a decision in it down into
`domain/` where it is tested normally.

The exception worth knowing: `domain/reminders.ts` is unit tested end to end
specifically because five consecutive ignores takes five days to reproduce by
hand, so the smoke checklist *cannot* be its acceptance basis.

## The size ceiling

NFR-6 caps `src/` at **2,000 effective lines**, counted like this:

```sh
find src -name '*.ts' | xargs cat | grep -vE '^\s*$' | grep -vE '^\s*(//|/\*|\*|\*/)' | wc -l
```

At the time of writing that returns **1,958**, so 42 remain.

The ceiling is not a style preference. It is a tripwire: the project rejected a
Mini App, MET-based scoring, an individual leaderboard, per-user timezones and a
sport taxonomy (`SPEC.md` §8), and every one of those is the kind of feature that
arrives back as "just a small addition". Passing 2,000 lines is the signal that
one of them has crept back in.

There is no room for a feature here, only for fixes. **The next change that needs
more than 42 lines is a conversation about the ceiling, not a quiet overrun.**

::: {.callout-warning}
## Counting it the same way each time

Earlier figures in the build ledgers (1,186, then 1,510) were taken by hand and
run 10 to 30 lines below what the command above reports. Compare like with like,
or you will read a jump in the trend that never happened.
:::

## Conventions you will trip over

- Imports carry explicit `.ts` extensions (`allowImportingTsExtensions`), and types
  are imported with `import type` (`verbatimModuleSyntax`).
- `strict` and `noUncheckedIndexedAccess` are on, so indexed access needs a guard
  or a justified `!`.
- There is no linter. `bunx tsc --noEmit` is the static check.
- Comments explain *why*, cite the requirement ID, and record what was considered
  and rejected. That density is the house style, and matching it is expected.
- No em dashes or en dashes anywhere, including message copy.
